las found that his wife was  dead and that she had left behind her a  son of 15 years of age whose name was  Marco  and tis of him that this book tells the  two brothers abode at Venice a couple of  years tarrying until a pope should be  made when the two brothers had tarried  as long as I have told you and saw that  never a Pope was made they said that  their return to the great card must be  put off no longer so they set out from  Venice taking mark along with them and  went straight back to Accra where they  found the Leggett of whom we have spoken  they had a good deal of discourse with  him concerning the matter and asked his  permission to go to Jerusalem to get  some oil from the lamp on the Sepulcher  to carry with them to the great car as  he had enjoined the leg at giving them  leave they went from Accra to Jerusalem  and got some of the oil and then returns  to Accra and went to the leggett and  said to him as we see no sign of a Pope  being made we desire to return to the  great calm but we have already tarried  long and there has been more than enough  delay to which the leggett replied since  it is your wish to go back I am well  content wherefore he caused letters to  be written for delivery to the great car  bearing testimony that the two brothers  had come in all good faith to accomplish  his charge but that as there was no Pope  they had been unable to do so  [Music]  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

